---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Container from "../../components/shell/Container.astro";
import Section from "../../components/content/Section.astro";
import ContentDocument from "../../components/content/ContentDocument.astro";
import { getStaticOgImagePath } from "../../lib/og-image";
import { withAutoOgImage } from "../../lib/seo";

const seo = withAutoOgImage(
  {
    title: "Colophon · Webxsid",
    description: "Notes on how this site is built and maintained.",
    canonical: "/colophon",
  },
  getStaticOgImagePath(),
);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Colophon · Webxsid"
  description="Notes on how this site is built and maintained."
  seo={seo}
  backHref="/"
>
  <Container>
    <div class="py-8 lg:py-14">
      <div class="max-w-4xl space-y-14 lg:space-y-16">
        <Section title="Colophon">
          <div class="space-y-7">
            <p class="ui-lead max-w-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-text">
              A compact record of how this site is built, what constraints shape
              it, and which tools are doing the real work.
            </p>
            <ContentDocument class="mt-2">
              <h2>Technology</h2>

              <ul>
                <li>
                  <strong>Astro</strong> is the foundation of the site. I wanted something
                  that treated content as content, not another React application pretending
                  to be one.
                </li>

                <li>
                  <strong>TypeScript</strong> keeps the content model, build tooling,
                  and page generation honest as the site grows.
                </li>

                <li>
                  <strong>Tailwind CSS v4</strong> handles almost all of the styling.
                  Beyond a handful of shared components, most of the UI is just utilities
                  and typography.
                </li>

                <li>
                  <strong>Astro Content Collections</strong> and
                  <strong>Zod</strong> keep every content type using explicit, typed
                  schemas instead of relying on convention.
                </li>

                <li>
                  Everything is written in <strong>Markdown</strong> by default.
                  <strong>MDX</strong> only appears when a page genuinely benefits
                  from interactive components.
                </li>

                <li>
                  <strong>React</strong> is only used where interaction makes sense.
                  Most pages never hydrate any client-side JavaScript.
                </li>

                <li>
                  <strong>Cloudflare Pages</strong> hosts the site as static files.
                  That keeps deployment simple and close to the shape of what actually
                  gets built.
                </li>
              </ul>

              <h2>Design Principles</h2>

              <ul>
                <li>Static unless there's a good reason not to be.</li>
                <li>Markdown before databases.</li>
                <li>Client-side JavaScript earns its place.</li>
                <li>Fast before clever.</li>
                <li>Content first, animation second.</li>
                <li>Stable URLs should stay stable.</li>
                <li>Readable without JavaScript wherever possible.</li>
                <li>Progressive enhancement over framework magic.</li>
                <li>
                  If something feels decorative, it probably doesn't belong.
                </li>
              </ul>

              <h2>Design</h2>

              <p>
                I spend most of my day reading code, documentation, logs, and
                terminal output. Unsurprisingly, that's the kind of interface I
                enjoy spending time in.
              </p>

              <p>
                I wanted this site to feel closer to a notebook than a product
                landing page. Typography, spacing, and structure do most of the
                work. If the design disappears while you're reading, it's doing
                exactly what I hoped it would.
              </p>

              <h2>Implementation Details</h2>

              <ul>
                <li>
                  Open Graph images are generated automatically, including
                  reading time for writing pages.
                </li>
                <li>
                  Metadata is generated centrally so canonical URLs, Open Graph
                  tags, RSS, and structured data all stay in sync.
                </li>
                <li>
                  Writing, projects, work, references, and now pages all flow
                  through content collections with typed frontmatter rather than
                  ad-hoc parsing.
                </li>
                <li>
                  The theme system is driven by CSS variables, multiple theme
                  families, and an early bootstrap script so theme choice
                  applies before the page settles.
                </li>
                <li>
                  RSS and sitemap output are generated from the same content
                  sources as the pages, so syndication and indexing do not need
                  separate maintenance.
                </li>
                <li>
                  The site builds a small internal link and reference graph from
                  content bodies, which powers connected writing, external
                  references, preview data, and resource hints.
                </li>
                <li>
                  A few small scripts automate creating new entries, keeping
                  lists in sync, and handling content mirrors so writing stays
                  file-first.
                </li>
              </ul>

              <h2>Credits</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <strong>Astro</strong> for making content-first websites fun again.
                </li>

                <li>
                  <strong>Tailwind CSS</strong> for staying out of the way.
                </li>

                <li>
                  <strong>Cloudflare Pages</strong> for hosting that I rarely have
                  to think about.
                </li>

                <li>
                  <strong>Lucide</strong> for the icons scattered throughout the site.
                </li>

                <li>
                  <strong>Sharp</strong> for the Open Graph image pipeline.
                </li>

                <li>
                  <strong>IBM Plex</strong> for giving the site most of its voice.
                </li>
              </ul>
            </ContentDocument>
          </div>
        </Section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Container>
</BaseLayout>
